    $ua83c4cb7${"format":"udebt-analysis","version":1,"contract":1,"repo":"mlflow","url":"https://github.com/mlflow/mlflow","rev":"f6ff35427d7e1134a83f8f9d8215d0049149162f","sha":"f6ff35427d7e1134a83f8f9d8215d0049149162f","generated_at":"2026-11-15T09:59:44+00:00","seconds":704.9,"limits":{"max_commits":20000,"max_mb":2000,"clone_timeout":900},"clone_bytes":1563970560,"map_source":"llm","llm_calls":15,"code_excerpts":false,"counts":{"commits":14477,"contributors":1288,"units":30,"walkthroughs":12,"tour":true},"files":{"weights.json":3555434,"structure.json":33308,"history.json":29193,"contributors.json":388785,"map.json":51150,"specs.json":108584},"note":null,"public":true}$ua83c4cb7$::jsonb,
    null,
    $u2352cfaa${"version":3,"generated_at":"2026-11-15T09:52:01+00:00","head":"f6ff35427d7e1134a83f8f9d8215d0049149162f","tour":{"overview":"MLflow records machine-learning and LLM-application work and serves it back to users. The Python tracking client and fluent API is what users call. It reaches the Tracking server, REST API and protobuf schema, or goes straight to the Tracking & registry metadata stores. Artifact repositories hold large files, and Model flavors, pyfunc and serving package and serve models. Tracing SDK and exporters, Autologging and framework integrations, GenAI evaluation, scorers and judges, and the AI Gateway cover LLM work. Auth and server security protects the server, and Packaging, config and cross-cutting runtime builds and configures it all. The React web UI sits on top of the server.","sections":[{"unit_id":"artifact-repos","paragraph":"Artifact repositories store and fetch the large files a run produces, such as models and plots. The storage backend is chosen from the location's URI scheme. The unit uses the tracking server, metadata stores, client, packaging and tracing, and nearly every other unit uses it to read or write files. The decision was one small plugin per scheme, resolved from a registry, with the server able to proxy artifacts for clients. A single storage library such as fsspec was not adopted. The history does not say why. The split from tracking metadata came in the 2019 store reorganisation, written by a human, Mani Parkhe. The commit history shows no AI involvement in that origin."},{"unit_id":"ai-gateway","paragraph":"The AI Gateway is a proxy that hides differences between language-model providers. It handles endpoint configuration, guardrails, budgets and tracing. It uses the metadata stores, client, tracing, model serving, tracking server, evaluation and artifacts, and is used by evaluation, the server, autologging and others. Its design is a FastAPI gateway with one adapter per provider. Endpoints and secrets now live in the database and are served by the tracking server. A pure LiteLLM proxy or YAML-only configuration was not chosen, and the history does not say why. Harutaka Kawamura, a human, migrated it in 2023 and renamed it in 2024 from the deployments server. In 2026 TomuHirata added the guardrail database layer with AI assistance."},{"unit_id":"autologging-integrations","paragraph":"Autologging records parameters, metrics, models and traces from machine-learning libraries without users adding logging calls. It uses model flavors, tracing, the client, stores, packaging, evaluation, the gateway, artifacts and the server. The client, evaluation, flavors, tracing, the gateway and packaging use it in turn. The decision was to patch each library at runtime, wrap the patches so failures are tolerated, and keep a central table of supported library versions. Explicit callbacks only, or library-native plugins, were not chosen. The history does not give the reasoning, though the aim appears to be protecting user training code. A human, dbczumar, introduced the safety utilities in December 2020. No AI authorship appears there."},{"unit_id":"platform-config-packaging","paragraph":"This unit covers cross-cutting runtime and build concerns: environment-variable configuration, the separate package distributions, telemetry, background jobs, deployment charts and Databricks integration. It uses the stores, evaluation, client, server, flavors, artifacts, tracing and autologging, and most of those units also use it. All configuration comes from environment variables, and full, skinny and tracing distributions are built from one source tree. Configuration files and independently versioned packages were not chosen. The history only hints at why: a skinny client avoids heavy dependencies, and Databricks is a major host. Humans wrote the key changes: eedeleon the 2021 skinny client, Yuki Watanabe the 2025 tracing build, and WeichenXu the 2026 Helm charts."},{"unit_id":"genai-eval-scorers","paragraph":"This is the toolkit for evaluating LLM applications: an evaluation harness, scorers, LLM judges, datasets and assessments. It uses the stores, client, tracing, server, packaging, gateway, flavors, artifacts and autologging, and is used by the stores, server, client, gateway, tracing and others. Scorers are serializable objects saved in experiments, judges call models through adapters such as litellm or the gateway, and third-party metrics are wrapped. Relying only on the older metrics and evaluate features was not chosen, reflecting the move from classic model evaluation to LLM evaluation. Humans WeichenXu, Ben Wilson and Corey Zumar built most of it in 2025 and 2026. Xiang Shen's 2026 removal of the Databricks serving adapter was AI-assisted."},{"unit_id":"tracing","paragraph":"The tracing SDK records LLM and application execution as traces, with decorators, span processing and export to MLflow or OTLP backends. It uses the stores, client, packaging, artifacts, server, evaluation, autologging, gateway and flavors, and those units use it in return. It is built on OpenTelemetry with a private tracer provider and MLflow-specific processors and exporters, using the V3 trace schema. A custom span model or the global OpenTelemetry provider were not chosen. The aims appear to be compatibility with OpenTelemetry and isolation from user instrumentation, though the history does not state this. Yuki Watanabe, a human, led it, merging the feature branch in 2025 after earlier and later refinements."},{"unit_id":"auth-security","paragraph":"Auth and server security provides optional basic authentication with per-resource permissions, plus host and CORS protections for the server. It uses the server, client, stores, packaging, tracing, artifacts, gateway and evaluation, and is used by the tracking server. It is a server plugin with its own SQLAlchemy store. List queries are scoped before reaching storage, and no default admin password ships. External identity providers only, or authentication at the gateway, were not chosen, and the history does not say why. A security advisory led to removing the default password, which Haoji Tang, a human, did in 2026. Debarati Basu-Nag's later query-scoping refactor was AI-assisted."},{"unit_id":"rest-server-protos","paragraph":"The tracking server exposes the REST API that Python, Java, R, TypeScript and the web UI all use, with a protobuf schema defining the endpoints and messages. It uses the stores, evaluation, packaging, client, gateway, tracing, artifacts, auth and flavors, and is used by many of them in turn. Protobuf definitions generate Python and Java messages and drive request routing. The server is a Flask app with FastAPI sub-apps. OpenAPI-first, GraphQL-only and gRPC were not chosen. Proto-first gives every language one contract, but the history does not show why Flask and FastAPI are mixed. Humans shaped it: Mani Parkhe's 2019 store reorganisation and Yuki Watanabe's 2025 tracing branch merge."},{"unit_id":"tracking-fluent-client","paragraph":"The Python client is the API users call directly, such as starting a run and logging parameters or metrics. It hides store selection behind URIs. It uses the stores, packaging, tracing, artifacts, server, evaluation, autologging, flavors and gateway, and is used by evaluation, tracing, autologging, flavors and others. A fluent API with global active-run state sits on a client object and wrappers for the tracking and registry services. Neither the code nor the history shows which alternatives were considered or why this one won. A human, Matei Zaharia, wrote it in the initial commit of June 2018. Later changes are not traced in this unit's history."},{"unit_id":"tracking-store","paragraph":"The metadata stores persist experiments, runs, traces, the model registry, datasets and gateway entities behind one interface, whatever the backend. They use the server, tracing, evaluation, packaging, client, artifacts, flavors and gateway, and nearly every other unit uses them. The design is abstract store interfaces with SQLAlchemy and Alembic migrations as the main backend, a legacy file store, and a REST store for remote clients, selected by URI scheme. A single fixed database, raw SQL, a document store or files only were not chosen, to allow zero-config local use and scalable servers. Humans wrote the decisive changes: Mani Parkhe in 2019, then Yuki Watanabe, Ben Wilson and Corey Zumar. A 2026 gateway guardrail commit was AI-assisted."},{"unit_id":"models-pyfunc","paragraph":"This unit packages models in the MLmodel format with signatures and environment capture. A generic Python function interface and a scoring server let any model be loaded and served. It uses the client, server, packaging, artifacts, stores, gateway, autologging, tracing and evaluation, and is used by autologging, evaluation, packaging, the client and others. Each library gets its own flavor module, all flavors converge on the generic interface, and serving runs on gunicorn or FastAPI with captured requirements. ONNX-only or a single serialization format were not chosen, keeping deployment framework-agnostic. Humans shaped it: tomasatdatabricks switched serving to gunicorn in 2019, and Aravind Segu added the user auth policy in 2025."},{"unit_id":"web-ui","paragraph":"The web UI is a large React and TypeScript single-page app, served by the server, for browsing experiments, traces, the registry, the gateway and more. In this map it has no uses or used-by links. It changes together with the tracking server, the AI Gateway, and evaluation, and mostly consumes the REST endpoints. It is one app with shared packages, Redux plus react-query, and the Databricks design system. A server-rendered UI or a separate UI repository were not chosen. Sharing components with the Databricks product seems the reason, but the history leaves the details unclear. Humans wrote the sampled changes: Zhidong Qu in 2020 on model version schema display, and Daniel Lok in 2026 on lint configuration."}],"basis":"ec07bac34f44","head":"f6ff35427d7e1134a83f8f9d8215d0049149162f","generated_at":"2026-11-15T09:53:00+00:00"},"units":[{"id":"tracking-store","name":"Tracking & registry metadata stores","kind":"module","summary":"Pluggable store layer persisting experiments, runs, traces, registry, datasets and gateway entities via SQLAlchemy, file store or REST.","decision":"Abstract store interfaces with SQLAlchemy (Alembic-migrated) as the main backend, FileStore legacy, and REST store for remote clients; stores are selected by URI scheme.","alternatives":"Single fixed DB; ORM-free SQL; document store; only file-based storage.","why":"Supports zero-config local use and scalable server deployments; the 2019 store reorg split metadata from artifacts. Trace V3 schema migration moved traces into the stores.","paths":["mlflow/store/tracking","mlflow/store/model_registry","mlflow/store/db","mlflow/store/db_migrations/versions","mlflow/entities","mlflow/store/tracking/sqlalchemy_store.py","mlflow/store/tracking/dbmodels/models.py","mlflow/utils/search_utils.py","mlflow/store/db/utils.py","mlflow/db.py","mlflow/store/tracking/file_store.py","mlflow/store/tracking/abstract_store.py","fs2db","mlflow/entities/model_registry"],"centrality":5,"clusters":["C1.5","C1.7","C1.1"],"scope":"architectural","history":[{"sha":"60c71fa","date":"2019-10-03","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"Mani Parkhe","subject":"Reorg of \"mlflow.store\" module and splitting \"tracking\" metadata and \"artifact\" repository submodules (#1900)"},{"sha":"c0ca384","date":"2025-06-23","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"Yuki Watanabe","subject":"Migrate SQLAlchemyStore to use V3 trace schema (#16381)"},{"sha":"3e1de0c","date":"2025-06-25","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"Yuki Watanabe","subject":"Migrate FileStore to use V3 trace schema (#16382)"},{"sha":"3f90e98","date":"2025-09-04","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"Ben Wilson","subject":"Add Evaluation Datasets to MLflow (#17447)"},{"sha":"bdc041e","date":"2026-01-07","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"Corey Zumar","subject":"[Online Scoring][2/x] Add schema migration for online_scoring_configs table (#19708)"},{"sha":"4daba48","date":"2026-04-03","klass":"assisted","agent":"claude-code","author":"TomuHirata","subject":"Add gateway guardrail DB layer: schema, store methods, and tests (#21960)"}],"importance":0.733,"orientation":{"purpose":"The persistence layer for experiments, runs, traces, model registry, datasets and gateway entities. It gives the rest of the system one interface to save and query metadata, whatever the backend.","decision":"Abstract store interfaces with SQLAlchemy (Alembic-migrated) as the main backend, a legacy FileStore, and a REST store for remote clients, chosen by URI scheme. Alternatives were a single fixed DB, raw SQL, a document store or files only. The aim was zero-config local use plus scalable servers.","where":[{"path":"mlflow/store/tracking/abstract_store.py","what":"the store interface"},{"path":"mlflow/store/tracking/sqlalchemy_store.py","what":"main SQL backend"},{"path":"mlflow/store/db_migrations/versions","what":"Alembic schema migrations"}],"shaped_by":"Humans alone wrote the decisive commits: Mani Parkhe's 2019 store reorg, then Yuki Watanabe, Ben Wilson and Corey Zumar on V3 traces, datasets and scoring; a 2026 gateway guardrail commit was AI-assisted (TomuHirata).","basis":"10feeee0a03c","head":"f6ff35427d7e1134a83f8f9d8215d0049149162f","generated_at":"2026-11-15T09:52:30+00:00"},"children":[{"id":"tracking-store/sqlalchemy-store","name":"SQLAlchemy store and models","kind":"module","summary":"Main DB-backed tracking store with ORM models and search translation.","decision":"SQLAlchemy ORM with search filter parsing in search_utils.","paths":["mlflow/store/tracking/sqlalchemy_store.py","mlflow/store/tracking/dbmodels/models.py","mlflow/utils/search_utils.py"],"centrality":5,"scope":"architectural","history":[],"importance":0.276},{"id":"tracking-store/migrations","name":"Alembic migrations","kind":"boundary","summary":"Schema evolution via many Alembic revisions.","decision":"Every schema change ships an Alembic revision file.","paths":["mlflow/store/db_migrations/versions","mlflow/store/db/utils.py","mlflow/db.py"],"centrality":4,"scope":"architectural","history":[],"importance":0.174},{"id":"tracking-store/file-store","name":"FileStore and abstract store","kind":"module","summary":"Filesystem backend and abstract store interface.","decision":"Keep a file backend alongside DB (with an fs2db migration tool).","paths":["mlflow/store/tracking/file_store.py","mlflow/store/tracking/abstract_store.py","fs2db"],"centrality":3,"scope":"architectural","history":[],"importance":0.138},{"id":"tracking-store/registry-store","name":"Model/prompt registry store","kind":"module","summary":"Registry persistence for models, prompts and related entities.","decision":"Separate registry store hierarchy sharing the DB infrastructure.","paths":["mlflow/store/model_registry","mlflow/entities/model_registry"],"centrality":3,"scope":"architectural","history":[],"importance":0.145}]},{"id":"rest-server-protos","name":"Tracking server, REST API and protobuf schema","kind":"boundary","summary":"Flask/FastAPI server exposing proto-defined REST endpoints that all clients (Python, Java, R, TS, UI) use.","decision":"Protobuf definitions generate Python/Java messages and drive handler routing in a large handlers.py; server is a Flask app with FastAPI sub-apps.","alternatives":"OpenAPI-first; GraphQL only; gRPC.","why":"Proto-first gives multi-language clients a shared contract; unclear for the Flask+FastAPI mix.","paths":["mlflow/server/handlers.py","mlflow/protos","mlflow/server/__init__.py","mlflow/server/fastapi_app.py","dev/generate_protos.py","mlflow/store/tracking/rest_store.py","mlflow/utils/rest_utils.py"],"centrality":5,"clusters":["C1.2","C1.9"],"scope":"architectural","history":[{"sha":"60c71fa","date":"2019-10-03","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"Mani Parkhe","subject":"Reorg of \"mlflow.store\" module and splitting \"tracking\" metadata and \"artifact\" repository submodules (#1900)"},{"sha":"d8d92ca","date":"2025-05-01","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"Yuki Watanabe","subject":"Merge Tracing SDK feature branch (#15561)"}],"importance":0.691,"orientation":{"purpose":"The tracking server that exposes the REST API all clients use. A shared protobuf schema defines the endpoints and messages.","decision":"Protobuf definitions generate the Python and Java messages and drive routing in the large handlers.py. The server is a Flask app with FastAPI sub-apps. OpenAPI-first, GraphQL and gRPC were not chosen. Proto-first gives every language one contract; the code does not show why Flask and FastAPI are mixed.","where":[{"path":"mlflow/server/handlers.py","what":"endpoint handlers and routing"},{"path":"mlflow/protos","what":"proto definitions"},{"path":"mlflow/server/fastapi_app.py","what":"FastAPI layer"}],"shaped_by":"Human-written: Mani Parkhe's 2019 store reorg and Yuki Watanabe's 2025 tracing feature-branch merge.","basis":"21496ad8ab3f","head":"f6ff35427d7e1134a83f8f9d8215d0049149162f","generated_at":"2026-11-15T09:52:30+00:00"}},{"id":"tracking-fluent-client","name":"Python tracking client and fluent API","kind":"module","summary":"mlflow.start_run/log_* fluent API over MlflowClient that picks stores via URI.","decision":"Fluent global-state API layered on a client and tracking-service/registry-service wrappers.","paths":["mlflow/tracking","mlflow/__init__.py","mlflow/environment_variables.py"],"centrality":5,"clusters":["C2.3","C1.6"],"scope":"architectural","history":[{"sha":"7a69ba6","date":"2018-06-05","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"Matei Zaharia","subject":"Initial commit of mlflow"}],"importance":0.717,"orientation":{"purpose":"The Python client API users call directly, such as start_run and log_*. It hides store selection behind URIs.","decision":"A fluent API with global active-run state sits on top of MlflowClient and the tracking and registry service wrappers. Neither the code nor the history shows what alternatives were considered or why this was chosen.","where":[{"path":"mlflow/tracking","what":"fluent API, client and service wrappers"},{"path":"mlflow/__init__.py","what":"top-level exports"},{"path":"mlflow/environment_variables.py","what":"configuration variables"}],"shaped_by":"Human-written: Matei Zaharia's initial commit in June 2018.","basis":"68b9b22c05a8","head":"f6ff35427d7e1134a83f8f9d8215d0049149162f","generated_at":"2026-11-15T09:52:30+00:00"}},{"id":"tracing","name":"Tracing SDK and exporters","kind":"module","summary":"OpenTelemetry-based tracing: fluent decorators, span processors, exporters to MLflow/OTLP, trace entities.","decision":"Built on OpenTelemetry with a private tracer provider and MLflow-specific processors/exporters; V3 trace schema.","alternatives":"Custom span model; global OTel provider.","why":"OTel compatibility and isolation from user instrumentation; the SDK was merged from a feature branch.","paths":["mlflow/tracing","mlflow/entities/span.py","mlflow/entities/trace.py","mlflow/server/otel_api.py","mlflow/tracing/provider.py","mlflow/tracing/fluent.py","mlflow/tracing/processor","mlflow/tracing/export","mlflow/tracing/client.py","mlflow/store/tracking/databricks_rest_store.py","mlflow/tracing/otel"],"centrality":5,"clusters":["C4.1","C4.2","C4.5","C4.10"],"scope":"architectural","history":[{"sha":"d8d92ca","date":"2025-05-01","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"Yuki Watanabe","subject":"Merge Tracing SDK feature branch (#15561)"},{"sha":"f790471","date":"2024-06-25","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"Yuki Watanabe","subject":"Refactor `@trace_disabled` implementation (#12285)"},{"sha":"c55516d","date":"2025-01-23","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"Yuki Watanabe","subject":"Support timeout for a trace (#14288)"},{"sha":"0f4a5bb","date":"2025-07-16","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"Yuki Watanabe","subject":"Update build scripts to handle mlflow-tracing (#15860)"}],"importance":0.669,"orientation":{"purpose":"The SDK that records LLM and app execution as traces. It offers decorators, span processing and export to MLflow or OTLP backends.","decision":"Built on OpenTelemetry with a private tracer provider and MLflow-specific processors and exporters, using the V3 trace schema. Alternatives were a custom span model or the global OTel provider. The aims were OTel compatibility and isolation from user instrumentation.","where":[{"path":"mlflow/tracing/fluent.py","what":"decorators and fluent API"},{"path":"mlflow/tracing/provider.py","what":"private tracer provider"},{"path":"mlflow/tracing/export","what":"exporters"}],"shaped_by":"Human-written, mainly by Yuki Watanabe: the 2025 feature-branch merge, with earlier and later refinements.","basis":"21fcdd615182","head":"f6ff35427d7e1134a83f8f9d8215d0049149162f","generated_at":"2026-11-15T09:52:30+00:00"},"children":[{"id":"tracing/provider-fluent","name":"Provider and fluent API","kind":"module","summary":"Tracer provider setup and @trace/start_span APIs.","decision":"Isolated OTel provider with enable/disable.","paths":["mlflow/tracing/provider.py","mlflow/tracing/fluent.py"],"centrality":5,"scope":"architectural","history":[],"importance":0.228},{"id":"tracing/exporters-processors","name":"Processors and exporters","kind":"module","summary":"Convert spans to traces and ship them to stores/OTLP.","decision":"Pluggable processors per destination (MLflow, Databricks, inference table, OTel).","paths":["mlflow/tracing/processor","mlflow/tracing/export"],"centrality":4,"scope":"architectural","history":[],"importance":0.181},{"id":"tracing/trace-client","name":"Trace client and stores","kind":"module","summary":"Client for trace search/log across backends.","decision":"Separate TracingClient wrapping stores.","paths":["mlflow/tracing/client.py","mlflow/store/tracking/databricks_rest_store.py"],"centrality":4,"scope":"architectural","history":[],"importance":0.143},{"id":"tracing/otel-translation","name":"OTel semantic translation","kind":"module","summary":"Maps external OTel/GenAI semconv attributes to MLflow spans.","decision":"Translator classes per convention.","paths":["mlflow/tracing/otel","mlflow/server/otel_api.py"],"centrality":3,"scope":"architectural","history":[],"importance":0.116}]},{"id":"models-pyfunc","name":"Model flavors, pyfunc and serving","kind":"module","summary":"MLmodel packaging, signatures, pyfunc generic interface, scoring server and environment capture.","decision":"Flavor-per-library modules converge on pyfunc; models serve through a gunicorn/FastAPI scoring server with captured requirements.","alternatives":"ONNX-only; single serialization format.","why":"Framework-agnostic deployment; serving moved to gunicorn in 2019.","paths":["mlflow/pyfunc","mlflow/models","mlflow/types","mlflow/utils/environment.py","mlflow/utils/requirements_utils.py","mlflow/sagemaker","mlflow/deployments"],"centrality":5,"clusters":["C2.1","C2.2","C2.6","C2.7","C2.8"],"scope":"architectural","history":[{"sha":"9a8ee6c","date":"2019-05-24","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"tomasatdatabricks","subject":"Switch to gunicorn for serving of python models. (#1322)"},{"sha":"fa1c06c","date":"2025-02-18","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"Aravind Segu","subject":"Introduce User Auth Policy for Pyfunc Models (#14538)"}],"importance":0.787,"orientation":{"purpose":"Packages models in the MLmodel format with signatures and environment capture. The generic pyfunc interface and scoring server let any model be loaded and served.","decision":"Each library gets a flavor module, and all flavors converge on pyfunc. Serving uses a gunicorn/FastAPI scoring server with captured requirements. ONNX-only or a single serialization format were not chosen, to keep deployment framework-agnostic.","where":[{"path":"mlflow/pyfunc","what":"generic interface and scoring server"},{"path":"mlflow/models","what":"MLmodel, signatures, flavors"},{"path":"mlflow/utils/environment.py","what":"environment capture"}],"shaped_by":"Human-written: the 2019 gunicorn switch (tomasatdatabricks) and Aravind Segu's 2025 user auth policy.","basis":"42e5ee77a4d9","head":"f6ff35427d7e1134a83f8f9d8215d0049149162f","generated_at":"2026-11-15T09:52:30+00:00"}},{"id":"auth-security","name":"Auth and server security","kind":"decision","summary":"Optional basic-auth plugin with per-resource permissions, plus host/CORS security middleware.","decision":"Auth implemented as a server plugin with its own SQLAlchemy store; collection queries scoped before storage; no default admin password.","alternatives":"External IdP/OIDC only; gateway-level auth.","why":"Security advisory removed default password; refactor scopes list queries to avoid leaks.","paths":["mlflow/server/auth","mlflow/server/security.py","mlflow/server/fastapi_security.py","mlflow/server/security_utils.py"],"centrality":4,"clusters":["C1.8","C12.6"],"scope":"architectural","history":[{"sha":"cd5064d","date":"2026-09-11","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"Haoji Tang","subject":"Remove the default basic-auth admin password shipped in `basic_auth.ini` (GHSA-gq3w-7jj3-x7gr) (#25751)"},{"sha":"a392f5f","date":"2026-10-28","klass":"assisted","agent":"claude-code","author":"Debarati Basu-Nag","subject":"Refactor basic auth to scope collection queries before storage (#26090)"}],"importance":0.512,"orientation":{"purpose":"Optional basic authentication with per-resource permissions for the tracking server, plus host and CORS security middleware.","decision":"Auth is a server plugin with its own SQLAlchemy store; list queries are scoped before they reach storage, and no default admin password ships. External IdP/OIDC-only and gateway-level auth were not chosen. A security advisory prompted removing the default password, and the scoping refactor avoids leaks.","where":[{"path":"mlflow/server/auth","what":"auth plugin and its store"},{"path":"mlflow/server/security.py","what":"security middleware"},{"path":"mlflow/server/fastapi_security.py","what":"FastAPI security"}],"shaped_by":"Haoji Tang (human) removed the default password in 2026; Debarati Basu-Nag's query-scoping refactor was AI-assisted.","basis":"883eb86661f6","head":"f6ff35427d7e1134a83f8f9d8215d0049149162f","generated_at":"2026-11-15T09:52:30+00:00"}},{"id":"artifact-repos","name":"Artifact repositories","kind":"module","summary":"URI-scheme-dispatched artifact storage (S3, GCS, Azure, Databricks, local, HTTP).","decision":"Per-scheme ArtifactRepository plugins resolved from a registry; server can proxy artifacts.","alternatives":"Single storage SDK such as fsspec.","why":"Splitting artifacts from tracking metadata in the 2019 reorg.","paths":["mlflow/store/artifact","mlflow/tracking/artifact_utils.py","mlflow/utils/uri.py"],"centrality":4,"clusters":["C1.3"],"scope":"architectural","history":[{"sha":"60c71fa","date":"2019-10-03","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"Mani Parkhe","subject":"Reorg of \"mlflow.store\" module and splitting \"tracking\" metadata and \"artifact\" repository submodules (#1900)"}],"importance":0.53,"orientation":{"purpose":"Stores and fetches artifact files such as models and plots. It picks a storage backend from the URI scheme.","decision":"Per-scheme ArtifactRepository plugins are resolved from a registry, and the server can proxy artifacts. A single storage SDK such as fsspec was not used. Artifacts were split from tracking metadata in the 2019 reorg.","where":[{"path":"mlflow/store/artifact","what":"repository implementations and registry"},{"path":"mlflow/tracking/artifact_utils.py","what":"artifact helpers"},{"path":"mlflow/utils/uri.py","what":"URI parsing"}],"shaped_by":"Human-written: Mani Parkhe's 2019 store reorg.","basis":"735f25f6ce84","head":"f6ff35427d7e1134a83f8f9d8215d0049149162f","generated_at":"2026-11-15T09:52:30+00:00"}},{"id":"autologging-integrations","name":"Autologging and framework integrations","kind":"module","summary":"Per-library autolog patches (sklearn, langchain, openai, dspy, etc.) with safety wrappers and version gating.","decision":"Monkey-patching with safe_patch error tolerance and a central supported-version table.","alternatives":"Explicit callbacks only; library-native plugins.","why":"Autologging must never break user training code; safety utilities introduced early.","paths":["mlflow/utils/autologging_utils","mlflow/ml_package_versions.py","mlflow/openai","mlflow/langchain","mlflow/dspy","mlflow/sklearn","mlflow/anthropic","mlflow/gemini","mlflow/bedrock"],"centrality":4,"clusters":["C2.4","C2.5","C4.3","C4.4","C4.6","C4.8","C4.9"],"scope":"architectural","history":[{"sha":"fa21520","date":"2020-12-11","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"dbczumar","subject":"Introduce utilities for autologging error tolerance / safety (#3682)"}],"importance":0.595,"orientation":{"purpose":"Automatically logs params, metrics, models and traces from ML libraries without users adding logging calls.","decision":"Per-library monkey-patching wrapped in safe_patch error tolerance, with a central supported-version table. Explicit callbacks only and library-native plugins were not chosen. Autologging must never break user training code, so the safety utilities came early.","where":[{"path":"mlflow/utils/autologging_utils","what":"safe_patch and safety helpers"},{"path":"mlflow/ml_package_versions.py","what":"supported version table"},{"path":"mlflow/sklearn","what":"example integration"}],"shaped_by":"Human-written: dbczumar introduced the safety utilities in December 2020.","basis":"677b2b4946fe","head":"f6ff35427d7e1134a83f8f9d8215d0049149162f","generated_at":"2026-11-15T09:52:30+00:00"}},{"id":"genai-eval-scorers","name":"GenAI evaluation, scorers and judges","kind":"module","summary":"mlflow.genai evaluation harness, scorer abstraction, LLM judges, optimizers, datasets and assessments.","decision":"Scorers are serializable objects stored in experiments; judges call LLMs via adapters (litellm/gateway); third-party metrics wrapped.","alternatives":"Legacy mlflow.metrics/evaluate only.","why":"Shift from classic model eval to LLM-app eval; Databricks serving adapter dropped in favour of litellm.","paths":["mlflow/genai","mlflow/entities/assessment.py","mlflow/entities/scorer.py","mlflow/entities/evaluation_dataset.py","mlflow/models/evaluation","mlflow/metrics","mlflow/genai/scorers","mlflow/genai/evaluation","mlflow/genai/judges","mlflow/genai/datasets","mlflow/data"],"centrality":4,"clusters":["C3.1","C3.2","C3.4","C3.5","C3.6","C3.8","C3.10","C3.11","C8.1","C8.2"],"scope":"architectural","history":[{"sha":"ae8fabe","date":"2025-08-18","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"WeichenXu","subject":"Backend for storing scorers in MLflow Experiment (#17090)"},{"sha":"3f90e98","date":"2025-09-04","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"Ben Wilson","subject":"Add Evaluation Datasets to MLflow (#17447)"},{"sha":"6ed06c4","date":"2026-01-21","klass":"assisted","agent":"claude-code","author":"Xiang Shen","subject":"Remove databricks serving endpoint adapter and redirect databricks managed models to litellm adapter (#20037)"},{"sha":"bdc041e","date":"2026-01-07","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"Corey Zumar","subject":"[Online Scoring][2/x] Add schema migration for online_scoring_configs table (#19708)"}],"importance":0.645,"orientation":{"purpose":"The mlflow.genai toolkit for evaluating LLM applications. It covers the evaluation harness, scorers, LLM judges, datasets and assessments.","decision":"Scorers are serializable objects stored in experiments, and judges call LLMs through adapters (litellm or gateway). Third-party metrics are wrapped. Legacy mlflow.metrics/evaluate alone was not kept. The Databricks serving adapter was dropped in favour of litellm.","where":[{"path":"mlflow/genai/scorers","what":"scorer abstraction"},{"path":"mlflow/genai/judges","what":"LLM judges"},{"path":"mlflow/genai/evaluation","what":"evaluation harness"}],"shaped_by":"Mostly human-written (WeichenXu, Ben Wilson, Corey Zumar, 2025–26); the 2026 Databricks adapter removal by Xiang Shen was AI-assisted.","basis":"694cb4197cc3","head":"f6ff35427d7e1134a83f8f9d8215d0049149162f","generated_at":"2026-11-15T09:52:30+00:00"},"children":[{"id":"genai-eval-scorers/scorers","name":"Scorers and registry","kind":"module","summary":"Scorer base, built-in scorers, third-party wrappers, online scoring.","decision":"Scorer objects serialize and register to the backend.","paths":["mlflow/genai/scorers"],"centrality":4,"scope":"architectural","history":[],"importance":0.168},{"id":"genai-eval-scorers/evaluation-harness","name":"Evaluation harness","kind":"flow","summary":"mlflow.genai.evaluate runs predict, scorers and logs assessments.","decision":"Trace-centric parallel harness.","paths":["mlflow/genai/evaluation"],"centrality":4,"scope":"architectural","history":[],"importance":0.128},{"id":"genai-eval-scorers/judges","name":"Judges and adapters","kind":"module","summary":"LLM judge definitions, adapters, optimizers.","decision":"Adapter layer over litellm/gateway/Databricks.","paths":["mlflow/genai/judges"],"centrality":3,"scope":"architectural","history":[],"importance":0.123},{"id":"genai-eval-scorers/datasets","name":"Datasets and data sources","kind":"module","summary":"Evaluation datasets and dataset/source tracking.","decision":"Datasets persisted in tracking store.","paths":["mlflow/genai/datasets","mlflow/data"],"centrality":3,"scope":"architectural","history":[],"importance":0.097},{"id":"genai-eval-scorers/classic-eval","name":"Classic evaluate and metrics","kind":"module","summary":"mlflow.evaluate default evaluator and metric definitions.","decision":"Plugin evaluators.","paths":["mlflow/models/evaluation","mlflow/metrics"],"centrality":2,"scope":"architectural","history":[],"importance":0.13}]},{"id":"platform-config-packaging","name":"Packaging, config and cross-cutting runtime","kind":"decision","summary":"Env-var configuration, skinny/tracing split packages, telemetry, background jobs, deployment (Docker, Helm), Databricks integration.","decision":"All config via environment variables; multiple distributions (mlflow, skinny, tracing) built from one tree; Databricks-specific code paths throughout.","alternatives":"Config files; monorepo with independent packages.","why":"Skinny client avoids heavy deps; Databricks is the main commercial host.","paths":["mlflow/environment_variables.py","libs/skinny","libs/tracing","pyproject.toml","pyproject.release.toml","mlflow/telemetry","mlflow/server/jobs","mlflow/utils/databricks_utils.py","charts","docker","dev/build.py"],"centrality":4,"clusters":["C3.3","C9.1"],"scope":"architectural","history":[{"sha":"d1a2647","date":"2021-01-26","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"eedeleon","subject":"[SkinnyClient] Remove pandas and numpy dependency (#3972)"},{"sha":"0f4a5bb","date":"2025-07-16","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"Yuki Watanabe","subject":"Update build scripts to handle mlflow-tracing (#15860)"},{"sha":"86f39f8","date":"2026-05-02","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"WeichenXu","subject":"Add Helm charts for deploying mlflow to kubernetes cluster (#21973)"}],"importance":0.596,"orientation":{"purpose":"Cross-cutting runtime and build concerns: environment-variable configuration, the separate package distributions, telemetry, background jobs, deployment charts and Databricks integration.","decision":"All config comes from environment variables, and several distributions (full, skinny, tracing) are built from one tree. Config files and independent packages were not chosen. A skinny client avoids heavy dependencies, and Databricks is the main commercial host.","where":[{"path":"mlflow/environment_variables.py","what":"config variables"},{"path":"pyproject.toml","what":"packaging metadata"},{"path":"dev/build.py","what":"distribution build 