her",25.29],["ai-gateway","web-ui","together",11.85],["artifact-repos","auth-security","together",2.26],["artifact-repos","autologging-integrations","together",3.01],["artifact-repos","genai-eval-scorers","together",3.67],["artifact-repos","models-pyfunc","together",11.11],["artifact-repos","platform-config-packaging","together",17.23],["artifact-repos","rest-server-protos","together",34.33],["artifact-repos","tracing","together",3.08],["artifact-repos","tracking-fluent-client","together",32.48],["artifact-repos","tracking-store","together",12.32],["artifact-repos","web-ui","together",0.45],["auth-security","autologging-integrations","together",0.2],["auth-security","genai-eval-scorers","together",1.64],["auth-security","models-pyfunc","together",1.58],["auth-security","platform-config-packaging","together",7.37],["auth-security","rest-server-protos","together",22.44],["auth-security","tracing","together",2.02],["auth-security","tracking-fluent-client","together",5.6],["auth-security","tracking-store","together",5.43],["auth-security","web-ui","together",7.17],["autologging-integrations","genai-eval-scorers","together",15.1],["autologging-integrations","models-pyfunc","together",59.33],["autologging-integrations","platform-config-packaging","together",30.91],["autologging-integrations","rest-server-protos","together",3.09],["autologging-integrations","tracing","together",21.89],["autologging-integrations","tracking-fluent-client","together",51.95],["autologging-integrations","tracking-store","together",10.88],["autologging-integrations","web-ui","together",0.9],["genai-eval-scorers","models-pyfunc","together",114.1],["genai-eval-scorers","platform-config-packaging","together",41.99],["genai-eval-scorers","rest-server-protos","together",19.11],["genai-eval-scorers","tracing","together",17.23],["genai-eval-scorers","tracking-fluent-client","together",27.1],["genai-eval-scorers","tracking-store","together",46.27],["genai-eval-scorers","web-ui","together",10.79],["models-pyfunc","platform-config-packaging","together",40.38],["models-pyfunc","rest-server-protos","together",10.56],["models-pyfunc","tracing","together",7.54],["models-pyfunc","tracking-fluent-client","together",41.89],["models-pyfunc","tracking-store","together",14.65],["models-pyfunc","web-ui","together",1.67],["platform-config-packaging","rest-server-protos","together",41.09],["platform-config-packaging","tracing","together",21.41],["platform-config-packaging","tracking-fluent-client","together",130.59],["platform-config-packaging","tracking-store","together",28.89],["platform-config-packaging","web-ui","together",10.65],["rest-server-protos","tracing","together",17.23],["rest-server-protos","tracking-fluent-client","together",54.6],["rest-server-protos","tracking-store","together",92.56],["rest-server-protos","web-ui","together",20.4],["tracing","tracking-fluent-client","together",38.54],["tracing","tracking-store","together",87.22],["tracing","web-ui","together",7.61],["tracking-fluent-client","tracking-store","together",74.58],["tracking-fluent-client","web-ui","together",4.01],["tracking-store","web-ui","together",10.65]],"hubs":[],"order":["artifact-repos","ai-gateway","autologging-integrations","platform-config-packaging","genai-eval-scorers","tracing","auth-security","rest-server-protos","tracking-fluent-client","tracking-store","models-pyfunc","web-ui"],"tour":{"markdown":"# Architecture tour: mlflow\n\nWritten by `udebt tour` at `f6ff3542` on 2026-11-15; 12 units, about 10 minutes to read. Units come in the order to learn them: each after the units it uses.\n\nMLflow records machine-learning and LLM-application work and serves it back to users. The Python tracking client and fluent API is what users call. It reaches the Tracking server, REST API and protobuf schema, or goes straight to the Tracking & registry metadata stores. Artifact repositories hold large files, and Model flavors, pyfunc and serving package and serve models. Tracing SDK and exporters, Autologging and framework integrations, GenAI evaluation, scorers and judges, and the AI Gateway cover LLM work. Auth and server security protects the server, and Packaging, config and cross-cutting runtime builds and configures it all. The React web UI sits on top of the server.\n\n## The map\n\n*Uses*: its code imports the other unit's (strongest first). *Used by*: how many units import it. *Changes with*: the units commits most often touch with it.\n\n1. **Artifact repositories** (`artifact-repos`): uses `rest-server-protos`, `tracking-store`, `tracking-fluent-client`, `platform-config-packaging`, `tracing`; used by 10; changes with `rest-server-protos`, `tracking-fluent-client`\n2. **AI Gateway** (`ai-gateway`): uses `tracking-store`, `tracking-fluent-client`, `platform-config-packaging`, `tracing`, `models-pyfunc`, `rest-server-protos`, `genai-eval-scorers`, `artifact-repos`, `autologging-integrations`; used by 8; changes with `tracking-store`, `models-pyfunc`\n3. **Autologging and framework integrations** (`autologging-integrations`): uses `models-pyfunc`, `tracing`, `tracking-fluent-client`, `tracking-store`, `platform-config-packaging`, `genai-eval-scorers`, `ai-gateway`, `artifact-repos`, `rest-server-protos`; used by 6; changes with `models-pyfunc`, `tracking-fluent-client`\n4. **Packaging, config and cross-cutting runtime** (`platform-config-packaging`): uses `tracking-store`, `genai-eval-scorers`, `tracking-fluent-client`, `rest-server-protos`, `models-pyfunc`, `artifact-repos`, `tracing`, `autologging-integrations`; used by 10; changes with `tracking-fluent-client`, `genai-eval-scorers`\n5. **GenAI evaluation, scorers and judges** (`genai-eval-scorers`): uses `tracking-store`, `tracking-fluent-client`, `tracing`, `rest-server-protos`, `platform-config-packaging`, `ai-gateway`, `models-pyfunc`, `artifact-repos`, `autologging-integrations`; used by 9; changes with `models-pyfunc`, `tracking-store`\n6. **Tracing SDK and exporters** (`tracing`): uses `tracking-store`, `tracking-fluent-client`, `platform-config-packaging`, `artifact-repos`, `rest-server-protos`, `genai-eval-scorers`, `autologging-integrations`, `ai-gateway`, `models-pyfunc`; used by 10; changes with `tracking-store`, `tracking-fluent-client`\n7. **Auth and server security** (`auth-security`): uses `rest-server-protos`, `tracking-fluent-client`, `tracking-store`, `platform-config-packaging`, `tracing`, `artifact-repos`, `ai-gateway`, `genai-eval-scorers`; used by 1; changes with `rest-server-protos`, `platform-config-packaging`\n8. **Tracking server, REST API and protobuf schema** (`rest-server-protos`): uses `tracking-store`, `genai-eval-scorers`, `platform-config-packaging`, `tracking-fluent-client`, `ai-gateway`, `tracing`, `artifact-repos`, `auth-security`, `models-pyfunc`; used by 10; changes with `tracking-store`, `tracking-fluent-client`\n9. **Python tracking client and fluent API** (`tracking-fluent-client`): uses `tracking-store`, `platform-config-packaging`, `tracing`, `artifact-repos`, `rest-server-protos`, `genai-eval-scorers`, `autologging-integrations`, `models-pyfunc`, `ai-gateway`; used by 10; changes with `platform-config-packaging`, `tracking-store`\n10. **Tracking & registry metadata stores** (`tracking-store`): uses `rest-server-protos`, `tracing`, `genai-eval-scorers`, `platform-config-packaging`, `tracking-fluent-client`, `artifact-repos`, `models-pyfunc`, `ai-gateway`; used by 10; changes with `rest-server-protos`, `tracing`\n11. **Model flavors, pyfunc and serving** (`models-pyfunc`): uses `tracking-fluent-client`, `rest-server-protos`, `platform-config-packaging`, `artifact-repos`, `tracking-store`, `ai-gateway`, `autologging-integrations`, `tracing`, `genai-eval-scorers`; used by 8; changes with `genai-eval-scorers`, `autologging-integrations`\n12. **React web UI** (`web-ui`): changes with `rest-server-protos`, `ai-gateway`\n\n## 1. Artifact repositories (`artifact-repos`)\n\nArtifact repositories store and fetch the large files a run produces, such as models and plots. The storage backend is chosen from the location's URI scheme. The unit uses the tracking server, metadata stores, client, packaging and tracing, and nearly every other unit uses it to read or write files. The decision was one small plugin per scheme, resolved from a registry, with the server able to proxy artifacts for clients. A single storage library such as fsspec was not adopted. The history does not say why. The split from tracking metadata came in the 2019 store reorganisation, written by a human, Mani Parkhe. The commit history shows no AI involvement in that origin.\n\nOpen first: `mlflow/store/artifact`, `mlflow/tracking/artifact_utils.py`, `mlflow/utils/uri.py`.\n\n## 2. AI Gateway (`ai-gateway`)\n\nThe AI Gateway is a proxy that hides differences between language-model providers. It handles endpoint configuration, guardrails, budgets and tracing. It uses the metadata stores, client, tracing, model serving, tracking server, evaluation and artifacts, and is used by evaluation, the server, autologging and others. Its design is a FastAPI gateway with one adapter per provider. Endpoints and secrets now live in the database and are served by the tracking server. A pure LiteLLM proxy or YAML-only configuration was not chosen, and the history does not say why. Harutaka Kawamura, a human, migrated it in 2023 and renamed it in 2024 from the deployments server. In 2026 TomuHirata added the guardrail database layer with AI assistance.\n\nOpen first: `mlflow/gateway`, `mlflow/server/gateway_api.py`, `mlflow/utils/providers.py`.\n\n## 3. Autologging and framework integrations (`autologging-integrations`)\n\nAutologging records parameters, metrics, models and traces from machine-learning libraries without users adding logging calls. It uses model flavors, tracing, the client, stores, packaging, evaluation, the gateway, artifacts and the server. The client, evaluation, flavors, tracing, the gateway and packaging use it in turn. The decision was to patch each library at runtime, wrap the patches so failures are tolerated, and keep a central table of supported library versions. Explicit callbacks only, or library-native plugins, were not chosen. The history does not give the reasoning, though the aim appears to be protecting user training code. A human, dbczumar, introduced the safety utilities in December 2020. No AI authorship appears there.\n\nOpen first: `mlflow/utils/autologging_utils`, `mlflow/ml_package_versions.py`, `mlflow/sklearn`.\n\n## 4. Packaging, config and cross-cutting runtime (`platform-config-packaging`)\n\nThis unit covers cross-cutting runtime and build concerns: environment-variable configuration, the separate package distributions, telemetry, background jobs, deployment charts and Databricks integration. It uses the stores, evaluation, client, server, flavors, artifacts, tracing and autologging, and most of those units also use it. All configuration comes from environment variables, and full, skinny and tracing distributions are built from one source tree. Configuration files and independently versioned packages were not chosen. The history only hints at why: a skinny client avoids heavy dependencies, and Databricks is a major host. Humans wrote the key changes: eedeleon the 2021 skinny client, Yuki Watanabe the 2025 tracing build, and WeichenXu the 2026 Helm charts.\n\nOpen first: `mlflow/environment_variables.py`, `pyproject.toml`, `dev/build.py`.\n\n## 5. GenAI evaluation, scorers and judges (`genai-eval-scorers`)\n\nThis is the toolkit for evaluating LLM applications: an evaluation harness, scorers, LLM judges, datasets and assessments. It uses the stores, client, tracing, server, packaging, gateway, flavors, artifacts and autologging, and is used by the stores, server, client, gateway, tracing and others. Scorers are serializable objects saved in experiments, judges call models through adapters such as litellm or the gateway, and third-party metrics are wrapped. Relying only on the older metrics and evaluate features was not chosen, reflecting the move from classic model evaluation to LLM evaluation. Humans WeichenXu, Ben Wilson and Corey Zumar built most of it in 2025 and 2026. Xiang Shen's 2026 removal of the Databricks serving adapter was AI-assisted.\n\nOpen first: `mlflow/genai/scorers`, `mlflow/genai/judges`, `mlflow/genai/evaluation`.\n\n## 6. Tracing SDK and exporters (`tracing`)\n\nThe tracing SDK records LLM and application execution as traces, with decorators, span processing and export to MLflow or OTLP backends. It uses the stores, client, packaging, artifacts, server, evaluation, autologging, gateway and flavors, and those units use it in return. It is built on OpenTelemetry with a private tracer provider and MLflow-specific processors and exporters, using the V3 trace schema. A custom span model or the global OpenTelemetry provider were not chosen. The aims appear to be compatibility with OpenTelemetry and isolation from user instrumentation, though the history does not state this. Yuki Watanabe, a human, led it, merging the feature branch in 2025 after earlier and later refinements.\n\nOpen first: `mlflow/tracing/fluent.py`, `mlflow/tracing/provider.py`, `mlflow/tracing/export`.\n\n## 7. Auth and server security (`auth-security`)\n\nAuth and server security provides optional basic authentication with per-resource permissions, plus host and CORS protections for the server. It uses the server, client, stores, packaging, tracing, artifacts, gateway and evaluation, and is used by the tracking server. It is a server plugin with its own SQLAlchemy store. List queries are scoped before reaching storage, and no default admin password ships. External identity providers only, or authentication at the gateway, were not chosen, and the history does not say why. A security advisory led to removing the default password, which Haoji Tang, a human, did in 2026. Debarati Basu-Nag's later query-scoping refactor was AI-assisted.\n\nOpen first: `mlflow/server/auth`, `mlflow/server/security.py`, `mlflow/server/fastapi_security.py`.\n\n## 8. Tracking server, REST API and protobuf schema (`rest-server-protos`)\n\nThe tracking server exposes the REST API that Python, Java, R, TypeScript and the web UI all use, with a protobuf schema defining the endpoints and messages. It uses the stores, evaluation, packaging, client, gateway, tracing, artifacts, auth and flavors, and is used by many of them in turn. Protobuf definitions generate Python and Java messages and drive request routing. The server is a Flask app with FastAPI sub-apps. OpenAPI-first, GraphQL-only and gRPC were not chosen. Proto-first gives every language one contract, but the history does not show why Flask and FastAPI are mixed. Humans shaped it: Mani Parkhe's 2019 store reorganisation and Yuki Watanabe's 2025 tracing branch merge.\n\nOpen first: `mlflow/server/handlers.py`, `mlflow/protos`, `mlflow/server/fastapi_app.py`.\n\n## 9. Python tracking client and fluent API (`tracking-fluent-client`)\n\nThe Python client is the API users call directly, such as starting a run and logging parameters or metrics. It hides store selection behind URIs. It uses the stores, packaging, tracing, artifacts, server, evaluation, autologging, flavors and gateway, and is used by evaluation, tracing, autologging, flavors and others. A fluent API with global active-run state sits on a client object and wrappers for the tracking and registry services. Neither the code nor the history shows which alternatives were considered or why this one won. A human, Matei Zaharia, wrote it in the initial commit of June 2018. Later changes are not traced in this unit's history.\n\nOpen first: `mlflow/tracking`, `mlflow/__init__.py`, `mlflow/environment_variables.py`.\n\n## 10. Tracking & registry metadata stores (`tracking-store`)\n\nThe metadata stores persist experiments, runs, traces, the model registry, datasets and gateway entities behind one interface, whatever the backend. They use the server, tracing, evaluation, packaging, client, artifacts, flavors and gateway, and nearly every other unit uses them. The design is abstract store interfaces with SQLAlchemy and Alembic migrations as the main backend, a legacy file store, and a REST store for remote clients, selected by URI scheme. A single fixed database, raw SQL, a document store or files only were not chosen, to allow zero-config local use and scalable servers. Humans wrote the decisive changes: Mani Parkhe in 2019, then Yuki Watanabe, Ben Wilson and Corey Zumar. A 2026 gateway guardrail commit was AI-assisted.\n\nOpen first: `mlflow/store/tracking/abstract_store.py`, `mlflow/store/tracking/sqlalchemy_store.py`, `mlflow/store/db_migrations/versions`.\n\n## 11. Model flavors, pyfunc and serving (`models-pyfunc`)\n\nThis unit packages models in the MLmodel format with signatures and environment capture. A generic Python function interface and a scoring server let any model be loaded and served. It uses the client, server, packaging, artifacts, stores, gateway, autologging, tracing and evaluation, and is used by autologging, evaluation, packaging, the client and others. Each library gets its own flavor module, all flavors converge on the generic interface, and serving runs on gunicorn or FastAPI with captured requirements. ONNX-only or a single serialization format were not chosen, keeping deployment framework-agnostic. Humans shaped it: tomasatdatabricks switched serving to gunicorn in 2019, and Aravind Segu added the user auth policy in 2025.\n\nOpen first: `mlflow/pyfunc`, `mlflow/models`, `mlflow/utils/environment.py`.\n\n## 12. React web UI (`web-ui`)\n\nThe web UI is a large React and TypeScript single-page app, served by the server, for browsing experiments, traces, the registry, the gateway and more. In this map it has no uses or used-by links. It changes together with the tracking server, the AI Gateway, and evaluation, and mostly consumes the REST endpoints. It is one app with shared packages, Redux plus react-query, and the Databricks design system. A server-rendered UI or a separate UI repository were not chosen. Sharing components with the Databricks product seems the reason, but the history leaves the details unclear. Humans wrote the sampled changes: Zhidong Qu in 2020 on model version schema display, and Daniel Lok in 2026 on lint configuration.\n\nOpen first: `mlflow/server/js/src/experiment-tracking`, `mlflow/server/js/src/shared/web-shared`, `mlflow/server/js/src/model-registry`.\n\n---\n\nNext: `udebt path` for the order to learn these in and the time each takes, `udebt dig <unit>` for a walkthrough of one with its code.\n","minutes":10},"walkthroughs":{"tracking-store":{"flow":"A user calls mlflow.start_run() with a tracking URI like sqlite:///mlflow.db. The call resolves a store from the URI scheme, opens the database, checks the schema and inserts a new run row.","steps":[{"file":"mlflow/tracking/_tracking_service/registry.py","start_line":44,"end_line":56,"narration":"The client asks the registry for a store. The URI is resolved first (None falls back to env vars or a default) and the result is cached by resolved URI, so every caller using the same URI shares one store instance. The builder is looked up by URI scheme and called with the URI and artifact root. This is the single point where the 'pluggable by URI scheme' decision is executed."},{"file":"mlflow/tracking/_tracking_service/utils.py","start_line":239,"end_line":255,"narration":"This is the scheme-to-builder table. An empty scheme or `file` maps to FileStore, `http` and `https` to the REST store, and every SQLAlchemy dialect to the SQL store, but only if sqlalchemy is importable. Entrypoints let third-party plugins add schemes. A new backend therefore means registering a builder here, not editing callers."},{"file":"mlflow/store/tracking/sqlalchemy_store.py","start_line":442,"end_line":470,"narration":"The SQL store's constructor gets a cached engine per URI. On a fresh, empty DB it creates the tables and runs the Alembic migrations, which is what gives zero-config local use. It then builds a session maker, with read-replica routing if configured, and finally verifies the schema. The artifact root is only recorded here, because metadata and artifacts were split in the 2019 reorg by Mani Parkhe."},{"file":"mlflow/store/db/utils.py","start_line":124,"end_line":135,"narration":"Schema verification compares the DB's Alembic revision with the single head revision in the migrations directory. On a mismatch the constructor raises and the store is never usable. The user is told to back up the DB and run `mlflow db upgrade`. This is why every schema change must ship an Alembic revision."},{"file":"mlflow/store/tracking/sqlalchemy_store.py","start_line":1084,"end_line":1108,"narration":"With a store in hand, create_run opens a write session and checks the experiment is active. It then builds the run's artifact location under the experiment's artifact root, de-duplicates tags and reconciles `run_name` with the `mlflow.runName` tag, raising if they conflict. The same behaviour is defined by AbstractStore, so FileStore and RestStore expose the same method."},{"file":"mlflow/store/db/utils.py","start_line":174,"end_line":188,"narration":"The `with` block in create_run is a managed session, and this code is what finishes it. After the block succeeds the session commits, so the `session.add(run)` becomes a row. MlflowException triggers a rollback and is re-raised. OperationalError becomes TEMPORARILY_UNAVAILABLE and other SQLAlchemy errors become BAD_REQUEST, so callers only ever see MLflow error codes."}],"trap":"A reader may assume the backend is chosen once per process and that the SQL store is the only real implementation. In fact each URI scheme maps to a different builder (file, REST, any SQL dialect or a plugin), and the instance is cached per resolved URI. The SQL store also refuses to start against an out-of-date schema instead of migrating it automatically. Only a completely empty database is initialized on its own.","head":"f6ff35427d7e1134a83f8f9d8215d0049149162f"},"rest-server-protos":{"flow":"A Python user calls mlflow.create_experiment, which the REST tracking store sends as POST /api/2.0/mlflow/experiments/create to a running MLflow server and which comes back as a CreateExperiment response with the new experiment id.","steps":[{"file":"mlflow/store/tracking/rest_store.py","start_line":308,"end_line":327,"narration":"The client-side store builds a CreateExperiment protobuf message and serializes it to JSON with message_to_json. It then calls _call_endpoint with the message class itself as the key. The proto class is the shared contract here, and the same class later selects the server handler."},{"file":"mlflow/utils/rest_utils.py","start_line":651,"end_line":660,"narration":"This is how the client finds a URL. It reads the same service descriptor, using the rpc option from the proto file, and maps each request class to a (path, HTTP method) pair. Nothing hard-codes `/experiments/create` in Python, so a path edited in the .proto file moves on both client and server."},{"file":"mlflow/utils/rest_utils.py","start_line":724,"end_line":776,"narration":"call_endpoint sends the JSON through http_request. Non-idempotent POSTs get restricted retry settings, and verify_rest_response raises if the status is not the expected one. A successful body is parsed back into the response proto with parse_dict, so the client sees the same typed message the server built."},{"file":"mlflow/server/handlers.py","start_line":8124,"end_line":8153,"narration":"On the server, get_service_endpoints walks the same service descriptors. For each endpoint it registers both `/api/...` and `/ajax-api/...` paths and looks up the handler by request class in the HANDLERS table, with _not_implemented as the fallback. This is the proto-driven routing from the decision. The 2025 tracing merge by Yuki Watanabe added many such v3 endpoints through this mechanism."},{"file":"mlflow/server/__init__.py","start_line":134,"end_line":135,"narration":"At import time every (path, handler, methods) tuple is registered on the Flask app with add_url_rule. The proto-generated routing table therefore ends up in plain Flask."},{"file":"mlflow/server/handlers.py","start_line":1897,"end_line":1921,"narration":"The handler is wrapped by catch_mlflow_exception and an artifacts-only guard. It parses and validates the request into a CreateExperiment message, calls the real tracking store, and serializes CreateExperiment.Response to JSON. An MlflowException raised anywhere in this function becomes a JSON error response, with the HTTP status taken from the exception's error code."}],"trap":"A reader may assume FastAPI serves the proto-defined tracking endpoints; in fact those routes are plain Flask views registered from the proto descriptors, and FastAPI (fastapi_app.py) only wraps the Flask app, mounted at \"/\" after its own routers, so its native routes take precedence.","head":"f6ff35427d7e1134a83f8f9d8215d0049149162f"},"tracking-fluent-client":{"flow":"A user calls mlflow.log_param(\"lr\", 0.01) with no run active, which implicitly starts a run and then writes the param to whichever store the tracking URI selects.","steps":[{"file":"mlflow/tracking/fluent.py","start_line":981,"end_line":983,"narration":"The fluent entry point holds no state about the backend. It asks for the active run (starting one if needed), decides sync vs async from an environment variable, and builds a brand-new MlflowClient for each call. This is where the 'global state' lives: only the run stack is global, and the client is cheap and stateless."},{"file":"mlflow/tracking/fluent.py","start_line":3577,"end_line":3581,"narration":"The implicit-run behaviour: if the thread-local run stack is empty, log_* silently calls start_run(). The same design from the original 2018 design by Matei Zaharia lets scripts log without ceremony, but it means any log call can create a run and hit the backend."},{"file":"mlflow/tracking/fluent.py","start_line":562,"end_line":590,"narration":"Inside start_run, the stack check rejects a second non-nested run, then a MlflowClient is created. The run ID can come from an argument or the MLFLOW_RUN_ID environment variable, in which case an existing run is fetched and resumed instead of a new one created. Env vars are therefore part of the control flow."},{"file":"mlflow/tracking/fluent.py","start_line":666,"end_line":672,"narration":"For a new run, context providers add tags (user, git, notebook, etc.) and the run is created through the client. The returned run is then pushed on the active run stack by the code after this, so later log_* calls find it."},{"file":"mlflow/tracking/client.py","start_line":280,"end_line":286,"narration":"MlflowClient resolves the tracking and registry URIs (argument, else global setting, else environment) and builds separate wrappers: a tracking service client, a tracing client and, lazily, a registry client. It is a facade; the URI is the only thing that determines which backend is used."},{"file":"mlflow/tracking/_tracking_service/client.py","start_line":88,"end_line":102,"narration":"TrackingServiceClient keeps only the URI. The store is a property that calls the URI-keyed store registry (cached by resolved URI), so the client stays serializable. The constructor touches the property once to fail early on a bad URI. log_param then calls store.log_param, the concrete file, SQL or REST implementation."}],"trap":"Readers assume the fluent API holds a global client or store connection, but only the active-run stack is global (thread-local). Each call builds a new MlflowClient, and the store is looked up from the URI through a cached registry.","head":"f6ff35427d7e1134a83f8f9d8215d0049149162f"},"tracing":{"flow":"A user calls a function decorated with @mlflow.trace; its root span is started, ended, and the resulting trace is exported to an MLflow tracking server.","steps":[{"file":"mlflow/tracing/fluent.py","start_line":317,"end_line":349,"narration":"The decorator's wrapper delegates to start_span and uses a generator so one body serves sync and async functions. Inputs are captured before the function runs and outputs after it returns, unless the user already called set_outputs. Everything is built on start_span, which is the real entry point to the SDK."},{"file":"mlflow/tracing/fluent.py","start_line":632,"end_line":662,"narration":"start_span asks the provider module for a raw OTel span. It then looks up the MLflow span that the processor has already registered, and applies span type, attributes and log level to it. Any failure in this block degrades to a NoOpSpan, so tracing never breaks user code. Spans dropped by the sampler also become no-ops that keep the OTel context, so children are dropped consistently."},{"file":"mlflow/tracing/provider.py","start_line":154,"end_line":201,"narration":"Here the 'private provider' decision shows up. By default get() returns MLflow's own isolated TracerProvider, so user or third-party OTel instrumentation is not mixed in. The shared global provider is used only when MLFLOW_USE_DEFAULT_TRACER_PROVIDER is false. get_or_init_tracer lazily initializes the provider exactly once on first use."},{"file":"mlflow/tracing/provider.py","start_line":739,"end_line":795,"narration":"Initialization builds the provider. In shared-global mode, if a TracerProvider already exists, MLflow's processors are added to it and nothing new is created, which gives unified traces. Otherwise it creates a new TracerProvider with an MLflow sampler and a Resource with MLflow SDK attributes, attaches the processors chosen by the destination, and stores it through provider.set. That call also retires the old provider's batch threads."},{"file":"mlflow/tracing/processor/base_mlflow.py","start_line":208,"end_line":281,"narration":"The processor is where OTel spans turn into MLflow traces. on_start creates the trace info for a root span and registers the MLflow span in the in-memory trace manager. on_end updates the trace info from the root span, then hands the span either to the batch delegate for async export or to the synchronous SimpleSpanProcessor path. Evaluation bypasses batching."},{"file":"mlflow/tracing/export/mlflow_v3.py","start_line":103,"end_line":152,"narration":"The exporter receives ReadableSpans. It first logs spans incrementally, grouped by experiment and workspace, and does this through an async queue when async logging is enabled. It then exports the full trace for root spans in _export_traces, which writes the V3 trace through TracingClient. Each root-span trace is popped from memory only when it is exported, and it is deferred while background child spans are still open."}],"trap":"A reader may assume the decorator ends by writing the trace to the store synchronously on the calling thread, or that spans live in the global OTel provider. In fact, the provider is private by default, and export normally goes through a batch processor and an async queue on background threads. Only evaluation or non-batch configuration exports synchronously.","head":"f6ff35427d7e1134a83f8f9d8215d0049149162f"},"models-pyfunc":{"flow":"A client POSTs a JSON payload to /invocations on a server started by `mlflow models serve`, and gets predictions from a model whose flavor is hidden behind pyfunc.","steps":[{"file":"mlflow/pyfunc/scoring_server/__init__.py","start_line":560,"end_line":586,"narration":"`get_cmd` builds the serving command. The model location is passed to the worker through an environment variable, not through the command line. The command today is `uvicorn ... mlflow.pyfunc.scoring_server.app:app`, so the 2019 gunicorn switch (tomasatdatabricks) has since been replaced by FastAPI/uvicorn. The request timeout also travels by environment variable."},{"file":"mlflow/pyfunc/scoring_server/app.py","start_line":1,"end_line":7,"narration":"Each uvicorn worker imports this module, which loads the model once at import time from the path in the environment variable. It then builds the FastAPI app around the model. A model that fails to load therefore stops the worker at startup, before any request is served."},{"file":"mlflow/pyfunc/__init__.py","start_line":1151,"end_line":1192,"narration":"`load_model` is where every flavor converges. It downloads the artifacts, warns if the captured requirements differ from the installed packages, and reads the MLmodel file. It then finds the `python_function` flavor config and imports the flavor's loader module by name (`conf[MAIN]`) to call its `_load_pyfunc`. This is why the server needs no knowledge of sklearn, torch or any other library."},{"file":"mlflow/pyfunc/scoring_server/__init__.py","start_line":441,"end_line":502,"narration":"`init` builds the FastAPI app around the loaded model. It reads the input schema from the MLmodel metadata and sets the serving-environment flag. A middleware turns slow requests into 504 responses. `/invocations` reads the body and the Content-Type, then runs the blocking `invocations` in a worker thread. MlflowExceptions are mapped to their own HTTP status codes."},{"file":"mlflow/pyfunc/scoring_server/__init__.py","start_line":278,"end_line":372,"narration":"`invocations` first validates the content type, accepting only UTF-8 and no extra parameters, and returns 415 otherwise. It then dispatches on CSV, JSON or Parquet to turn the body into a DataFrame or array. It calls `model.predict`, passing `params` only if the signature accepts it. Schema errors surface as MlflowException. Any other exception is wrapped as a generic BAD_REQUEST."},{"file":"mlflow/pyfunc/__init__.py","start_line":844,"end_line":905,"narration":"`PyFuncModel.predict` and `_predict` enforce the MLmodel signature. `_validate_prediction_input` coerces the data to the logged input schema and validates params. The call is then delegated to the flavor's own predict function. Signature enforcement happens here, in the generic layer, so it applies uniformly to all flavors."}],"trap":"A reader may assume that serving still runs on gunicorn, as in the 2019 commit, or that the server contains flavor-specific code. In the current code the server launches uvicorn on a FastAPI app and only ever calls the generic pyfunc `predict`, with the flavor loaded dynamically from the MLmodel file.","head":"f6ff35427d7e1134a83f8f9d8215d0049149162f"},"auth-security":{"flow":"A non-admin user's client sends a POST to the SearchExperiments REST endpoint on an MLflow server started with basic auth enabled.","steps":[{"file":"mlflow/server/security.py","start_line":73,"end_line":106,"narration":"Before any auth code runs, the security middleware's Flask before_request hooks check the request. One rejects a Host header that is not on the allowlist (DNS rebinding protection). The other rejects cross-origin state-changing API calls from origins that are not allowed. Both return 403 without touching credentials or storage. This layer is separate from the auth plugin and applies even when auth is off."},{"file":"mlflow/server/auth/__init__.py","start_line":10206,"end_line":10214,"narration":"create_app is how the auth plugin attaches itself. It registers the user and role routes and installs the auth before-request and after-request hooks on the Flask app. Under uvicorn it also wraps the app in a FastAPI app with a permission middleware. Auth is an opt-in layer around the existing server, not part of the core handlers, which is the plugin decision."},{"file":"mlflow/server/auth/__init__.py","start_line":6037,"end_line":6079,"narration":"Authentication runs next. It first accepts a delegation token. Otherwise it calls the configured authorization function, which defaults to basic auth. With no Authorization header it returns a 401 challenge. Otherwise it checks the credentials against the plugin's own SQLAlchemy store, optionally through a cache. It also refuses the legacy default admin password, the fix for the GHSA-gq3w-7jj3-x7gr advisory that Haoji Tang's commit shipped in 2026."},{"file":"mlflow/server/auth/__init__.py","start_line":6284,"end_line":6331,"narration":"_before_request is the gate. It skips unprotected routes, authenticates, and stores the username. Admins return early. Everyone else goes through the validator for that route, or the fail-closed branches when no decision exists. For a search route, the final lines look up a scoper and run it. Search is not allowed or denied as a whole. It is narrowed so the user only sees what they may read."},{"file":"mlflow/server/auth/__init__.py","start_line":3724,"end_line":3745,"narration":"This is the scoping from Debarati Basu-Nag's AI-assisted refactor. The scoper validates the user's filter first, so a comment cannot swallow the added clause. It then looks up the user's readable experiment IDs and appends an experiment_id IN/NOT IN clause to the request's filter. An empty grant list becomes a contradictory filter that matches nothing. The tracking store therefore only ever queries permitted rows, so there is no post-hoc filtering that could leak."},{"file":"mlflow/server/auth/__init__.py","start_line":3887,"end_line":3892,"narration":"This builds the lookup table that _before_request consults. It maps each (path, method) of the search and batch-get endpoints to its scoper, derived from the REST endpoint definitions. A new list endpoint is only scoped if it is added to the scoper registry that feeds this table. Otherwise it relies on a validator, or on fail-closed denial if that flag is on."}],"trap":"A reader may assume that list endpoints are protected by checking each returned row after the query, or that passing the permission check for a route means the user sees everything it returns. In fact, non-admin searches are rewritten before they reach the store, and admins skip validators and scopers entirely.","head":"f6ff35427d7e1134a83f8f9d8215d0049149162f"},"artifact-repos":{"flow":"A user calls MlflowClient().log_artifact(run_id, \"plot.png\") on a run whose artifact location is an mlflow-artifacts:/ URI, with the tracking URI pointing at an MLflow server.","steps":[{"file":"mlflow/tracking/_tracking_service/client.py","start_line":630,"end_line":658,"narration":"The tracking client does not know where a run's files live. It fetches the run's artifact URI from the tracking store, then asks the registry for a repository. The result is cached by run ID, so later calls skip the metadata lookup. The artifact side is only reached through the run's URI, which is how tracking and artifacts stayed separate after the 2019 reorg by Mani Parkhe."},{"file":"mlflow/store/artifact/artifact_repository_registry.py","start_line":60,"end_line":98,"narration":"This is the dispatch point. It enforces the host policy, takes the scheme from the URI, and looks it up in a dict. It then instantiates the registered class with the URI, tracking URI and registry URI, and wraps it for the workspace if that applies. An unknown scheme raises an error that lists the registered schemes. Policy is applied here so that indirect URIs such as runs:/ and models:/ follow the same rules."},{"file":"mlflow/store/artifact/artifact_repository_registry.py","start_line":120,"end_line":141,"narration":"The built-in schemes are registered at import time. Examples are s3, gs, wasbs, abfss, dbfs, http(s), runs, models and mlflow-artifacts, and an empty scheme maps to local. register_entrypoints then adds third-party plugins from the `mlflow.artifact_repository` entry point group. Supporting a new backend means adding a plugin, with no change to the core."},{"file":"mlflow/store/artifact/mlflow_artifacts_repo.py","start_line":58,"end_line":99,"narration":"This is the server-proxy case. The mlflow-artifacts scheme maps to a thin subclass of the HTTP repository. Its constructor rewrites the URI into an http(s) URL on the tracking server, using the tracking URI's host and the artifacts service root. It also rejects a tracking URI that is not http or https. The client never needs cloud credentials, because the server holds them."},{"file":"mlflow/store/artifact/http_artifact_repo.py","start_line":165,"end_line":185,"narration":"The repository's log_artifact validates the artifact path. It tries a multipart upload for large files and falls back to a plain PUT, unless the server only allows presigned uploads. The PUT sends the file to the server, which uses its own configured repository, such as S3 or local disk, to store it. Every repository implements this same interface, which is why the client code stays identical across backends."}],"trap":"A reader may assume the client always talks directly to S3, GCS or Azure based on the scheme. With an mlflow-artifacts URI the client only makes HTTP calls to the tracking server, and the server then runs its own scheme-dispatched repository.","head":"f6ff35427d7e1134a83f8f9d8215d0049149162f"},"autologging-integrations":{"flow":"A user calls mlflow.sklearn.autolog() and then runs model.fit(X, y) on a scikit-learn estimator.","steps":[{"file":"mlflow/utils/autologging_utils/__init__.py","start_line":406,"end_line":437,"narration":"The autologging_integration decorator wraps sklearn's autolog(). It stores the call's arguments as the integration's config, reverts any earlier patches, and returns early if disable=True. Every patch consults this stored config later, so each integration doesn't need its own disable logic. This is the first piece of the safety layer from dbczumar's 2020 change."},{"file":"mlflow/utils/autologging_utils/__init__.py","start_line":439,"end_line":465,"narration":"Warnings are rerouted and silenced according to `silent`. Then the version check runs before the real autolog body, so patches are only installed after the check. This is where the central version table is used, and it only warns."},{"file":"mlflow/utils/autologging_utils/__init__.py","start_line":364,"end_line":382,"narration":"An unsupported installed version only produces a logged warning. It never raises and never blocks patching, unless the user opts in with disable_for_unsupported_versions. It checks only the minimum version, because the table's maximum can't keep up with new releases."},{"file":"mlflow/sklearn/__init__.py","start_line":1122,"end_line":1151,"narration":"The sklearn integration installs each patch through safe_patch. This covers ordinary methods, properties, and sklearn's delegated methods, where the patch goes on the descriptor's fn. Unsupported method kinds are skipped quietly instead of failing."},{"file":"mlflow/utils/autologging_utils/safety.py","start_line":320,"end_line":404,"narration":"When the user calls fit, the call lands in safe_patch_function. It checks whether to skip autologging: the integration is disabled, a prior session failed, or exclusive mode is on with a user-created run active. If so, it calls the original untouched. Otherwise it goes on to run the patch inside a session."},{"file":"mlflow/utils/autologging_utils/safety.py","start_line":483,"end_line":523,"narration":"The patch function runs with a tracked wrapper around the original function. If the patch code throws, the session is marked failed and the error is swallowed, outside of test mode. The original is then still run if it hasn't been, and only a warning is logged. If the original itself throws, that exception reaches the user. This is how it avoids breaking user training code."}],"trap":"It is easy to think safe_patch swallows all exceptions during a patched call, but it only swallows those raised by MLflow's patch code. Exceptions from the user's original function are tracked with failed_during_original and re-raised, and in test mode patch errors are re-raised too.","head":"f6ff35427d7e1134a83f8f9d8215d0049149162f"},"genai-eval-scorers":{"flow":"A user calls mlflow.genai.evaluate(data, predict_fn, scorers=[an LLM-judge scorer]); each row is scored in parallel, the judge calls an LLM through an adapter, and the result is logged as an assessment on the row's trace.","steps":[{"file":"mlflow/genai/evaluation/base.py","start_line":338,"end_line":362,"narration":"The public evaluate() only delegates to _run_harness, which first validates scorers and parses quality criteria. This happens before any MLflow run is started, so bad input doesn't leave an empty run behind. The harness is trace-centric: predict_fn must emit a trace, and the scorers read from it."},{"file":"mlflow/genai/evaluation/harness.py","start_line":992,"end_line":1035,"narration":"For each row, every scorer runs through Scorer.run, optionally wrapped in a trace span, with rate limiting and retries. Any exception is caught and turned into a Feedback carrying a SCORER_ERROR rather than raised. One broken judge therefore never aborts the evaluation. Scorers for the row run in a thread pool capped by an env setting, because external LLM APIs rate-limit."},{"file":"mlflow/genai/scorers/base.py","start_line":994,"end_line":1018,"narration":"Scorer.run applies a timeout (a default unless the scorer sets its own). It then inspects the signature of the scorer's __call__ and passes only the arguments it declares (inputs, outputs, expectations, trace, session). This is why scorers, whether built-in, custom or serialized, can declare just what they need."},{"file":"mlflow/genai/judges/utils/invocation_utils.py","start_line":84,"end_line":100,"narration":"An LLM-judge scorer ends up in invoke_judge_model, which asks a factory for an adapter and returns the Feedback it produces. The adapter layer is where the 2026 change by Xiang Shen (AI-assisted) removed the Databricks serving endpoint adapter. Databricks-managed models are now redirected through litellm."},{"file":"mlflow/genai/judges/adapters/utils.py","start_line":53,"end_line":66,"narration":"The adapters are tried in priority order: Databricks-managed default judge, then native gateway providers, then LiteLLM as the catch-all. The first adapter whose is_applicable returns true wins. If none applies, a BAD_REQUEST error is raised. In the litellm adapter, applicability depends only on litellm being installed."},{"file":"mlflow/genai/evaluation/harness.py","start_line":1100,"end_line":1119,"narration":"The feedbacks are written to the row's trace as assessments. Each is stamped with the trace id, the source run id and the root span id, then logged via the tracking client. Results live on traces, not in a separate results table."}],"trap":"A reader may assume a judge always calls litellm, or that a failing judge fails the evaluation. In fact litellm is only the last fallback after the Databricks-managed and gateway adapters, and scorer exceptions are converted into error Feedback entries so the run continues.","head":"f6ff35427d7e1134a83f8f9d8215d0049149162f"},"platform-config-packaging":{"flow":"A developer sets MLFLOW_TRACKING_URI in the environment and imports mlflow, and a release engineer builds the mlflow-skinny wheel with dev/build.py.","steps":[{"file":"mlflow/environment_variables.py","start_line":13,"end_line":51,"narration":"This is the single config mechanism. Each variable is a small object holding a name, a type and a default. `get()` reads `os.environ` at call time, converts the string, and raises a ValueError naming the variable if conversion fails. There is no config file layer. Because it reads at call time, changing the environment mid-process takes effect."},{"file":"mlflow/environment_variables.py","start_line":63,"end_line":98,"narration":"Booleans get their own subclass with strict parsing. Only true/false/1/0, case-insensitively, are accepted, and anything else raises. This avoids the usual string-truthiness bugs when everything arrives as text. The block at lines 76-86 is a deprecation shim for a renamed variable."},{"file":"mlflow/environment_variables.py","start_line":101,"end_line":103,"narration":"A concrete declaration: the tracking URI is a string with default None. Callers elsewhere in the codebase import this object instead of calling `os.environ` directly. Documentation and defaults therefore live in one place. This is the first stop for anyone adding a setting."},{"file":"dev/build.py","start_line":22,"end_line":32,"narration":"One tree produces four build targets: dev, release, skinny and tracing. Each has a PyPI name and a build path. Skinny and tracing live in `libs/`, and the other two build from the repo root. The 2025 commit by Yuki Watanabe added tracing here."},{"file":"dev/build.py","start_line":110,"end_line":131,"narration":"The chosen package is built with `python -m build` on its own path. For the release type, `pyproject.release.toml` is copied over `pyproject.toml` first, and a context manager restores the file with `git restore` afterward. After the build, the wheel is checked for UI assets. This is where the 'one tree, several distributions' decision is enforced."},{"file":"libs/skinny/pyproject.toml","start_line":143,"end_line":147,"narration":"The skinny package includes the same top-level `mlflow` and `mlflow.*` packages as the full one. It differs only in its dependency list, which is generated by dev/pyproject.py. The tree is shared and the dependencies are trimmed. This is why server and UI code can sit in the same source tree while the skinny wheel stays light. The build script's post-check rejects UI assets in the skinny wheel."}],"trap":"A reader may assume skinny and tracing are separate codebases with their own source. They are separate metadata over the same `mlflow` source tree, so code that imports a heavy dependency at module level can break the skinny install.","head":"f6ff35427d7e1134a83f8f9d8215d0049149162f"},"ai-gateway":{"flow":"A caller POSTs a non-streaming chat request ({\"messages\": [...]}) to /gateway/<endpoint_name>/mlflow/invocations on the tracking server, and the gateway routes it to the endpoint's configured LLM provider.","steps":[{"file":"mlflow/server/gateway_api.py","start_line":800,"end_line":824,"narration":"The route captures the endpoint name from the URL and, in order, validates that the store is SQL-backed, loads the endpoint config from the DB, checks budgets, and loads guardrails. Budget rejection (429) therefore happens before any provider is built or any money is spent. This is the in-server design: no YAML file is read, and the handler runs on the tracking server's own store."},{"file":"mlflow/store/tracking/gateway/config_resolver.py","start_line":180,"end_line":211,"narration":"This is where DB-backed config replaces the old YAML config. It insists on a SqlAlchemyStore, checks a workspace-scoped cache, and otherwise reads the endpoint and its model mappings from the DB. Secrets are decrypted here, on the server only, which is why this is described as privileged. It is the first thing to change if config storage ever moves."},{"file":"mlflow/server/gateway_api.py","start_line":826,"end_line":837,"narration":"The body shape picks the path: 'messages' means chat, 'input' means embeddings. The payload is validated against the unified chat schema from mlflow/types/chat.py, and bad input becomes a 400. Then the provider is built from the endpoint name, after a System One check and SSRF protection setup."},{"file":"mlflow/server/gateway_api.py","start_line":583,"end_line":622,"narration":"Provider creation picks PRIMARY models. With traffic-split routing it builds a TrafficRouteProvider over weighted configs. Otherwise it takes the first primary model and looks up the provider adapter class by name. This is the per-provider adapter layer that hides provider differences from callers."},{"file":"mlflow/server/gateway_api.py","start_line":624,"end_line":666,"narration":"If the endpoint has fallback config, the primary provider is wrapped with a FallbackProvider holding the ordered fallback adapters. Fallback is independent of the routing strategy, so a traffic split can also have fallbacks. Callers see one provider interface either way."},{"file":"mlflow/server/gateway_api.py","start_line":876,"end_line":911,"narration":"The non-streaming call runs pre-LLM guardrails, then provider.chat, then post-LLM guardrails. All of it is wrapped in a traced call that records budget cost on completion. A guardrail violation becomes an HTTP 400. The streaming branch above skips post-LLM guardrails, which is a deliberate difference."}],"trap":"A reader may assume the gateway is a standalone service driven by a YAML config, as in the original deployments server. In this code it is a router on the tracking server, it reads endpoints and decrypted secrets from the database, and it only works with a SQL-backed store.","head":"f6ff35427d7e1134a83f8f9d8215d0049149162f"},"web-ui":{"flow":"A user opens the MLflow UI on an experiment page, and the SPA boots, routes to the experiment, and polls the tracking server's runs search endpoint for the latest issue-detection run.","steps":[{"file":"mlflow/server/js/src/index.tsx","start_line":1,"end_line":15,"narration":"The server serves one bundle, and this entry mounts a single root component into the page's root element. Nothing here is page-specific, so every feature (experiments, registry, gateway) is reached by client-side routing inside this one SPA. The global window error handler only logs, so a stray error does not blank the app."},{"file":"mlflow/server/js/src/app.tsx","start_line":44,"end_line":72,"narration":"The root waits for i18n to initialise and shows a skeleton until then. It then stacks the shared providers: Apollo, Intl, the Redux store, the Databricks design-system container with dark theme, the react-query client and server info, with the router innermost. This stack is the 'Redux plus react-query plus design system' decision made concrete. Every feature page inherits all of it."},{"file":"mlflow/server/js/src/MlflowRouter.tsx","start_line":242,"end_line":281,"narration":"Each feature folder contributes its own route definitions, and they are concatenated into one hash router. The gateway routes are only included when the server enables the gateway. The router is not built until feature flags have loaded, and a Suspense skeleton covers lazily loaded pages. Hash routing keeps all navigation client-side, so the server only has to serve the one page."},{"file":"mlflow/server/js/src/shared/web-shared/query-client/createMlflowQueryClient.ts","start_line":3,"end_line":16,"narration":"The react-query client is built here for the open-source entry points. It sets the network mode to 'always', so queries still run when the browser claims it is offline. The reason given is that the UI is served by the tracking server it talks to, so a page that loaded means the server is reachable. This is one of the few shared-library files that adapts the Databricks-shared code to the open-source deployment."},{"file":"mlflow/server/js/src/experiment-tracking/pages/experiment-issues/useIssueDetection.ts","start_line":70,"end_line":89,"narration":"A page-level hook uses react-query to POST to the REST endpoint 'ajax-api/2.0/mlflow/runs/search'. It sends the experiment id and a tag filter, and it passes the abort signal. The query key includes the workspace and the experiment id, so cached data is scoped to both. Retries are off, and polling every 3 seconds happens only while a detection run is in progress. This is the typical shape of a feature's data access: a REST proto endpoint wrapped in a query hook."},{"file":"mlflow/server/js/src/common/utils/FetchUtils.ts","start_line":48,"end_line":58,"narration":"All requests built on these helpers get default headers. These come from cookies named with the 'mlflow-request-header-' prefix and, when workspaces are enabled, an X-MLFLOW-WORKSPACE header. So authentication-proxy headers and workspace scoping are added in one place rather than in each feature. Changing the headers here affects every feature."}],"trap":"A reader may assume Redux is the single data layer, but newer features such as the issue-detection hook fetch REST endpoints through react-query and keep server state in the query cache. Redux is still mounted for older experiment components.","head":"f6ff35427d7e1134a83f8f9d8215d0049149162f"}}}$u1ac53ea8$::jsonb,
    null);
commit;
